  "notes": "The Tupelo duo's shared model: playful, hook-saturated party trap with a sparse kit and bright patches. Two things separate it from every other entry here. First, the kick is **sparse** — `densityPerBar` [2,4] with `syncopation` 0.4, the lowest pair in this batch — because the research says sparse kick placement and wide reverb on the claps, so the space is the sound; `layerClapOffsetMs` [3,9] is the widest clap spread here for the same reason. Second, `melody.intervals.step` is 0.7 and the register spans only 18 semitones, which is \"sung-chanted hooks with narrow range and high repetition\" turned into numbers. Marimba and bell timbres over two-chord beds (i–VI, i–VII) and static vamps. ⚠ `confidence` medium (research: high-medium). The INFERRED parameter is the 808: the research names two behaviours — long sustained with slow glides on the atmospheric cuts, punchier 16th repeats on the party cuts — and one `bass808` block cannot be both, so this entry authors the sustained-and-sliding reading (`legato`, `slideProb` 0.55) and leaves the punchy one to `slim-jxmmi`, which is where the party cuts live anyway. ⚠ `anticipationProb` raised to 0.15. With `mirror_kick` against a fully locked 808 both parts take every kick, so every bass onset lands exactly on an 808 onset — `coherence.rs` calls that one line played twice rather than two parts locking. The lock is the style and is untouched; the anticipation is what makes them two parts.",
